(1) Preliminary plat. Following a pre-submittal conference but before any grading, site development, or construction shall commence, the developer/subdivider shall submit a preliminary plat, as follows:
a. A preliminary plat and site plan which has been prepared in accordance with subsection (f) of this section, together with prescribed fee, shall be submitted to the City of Winder Planning and Development Department. Submission of the preliminary plat and supporting documents is required in electronic/digital format. The applicant may also be asked to provide paper copies upon request. The submission shall occur not less than fifteen (15) working days prior to the meeting date of the city at which the developer/subdivider desires preliminary plat review.
b. If access to a state highway is proposed, then GDOT shall receive copies of the proposed plat as stipulated by GDOT to determine the feasibility of such access in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 32-6-150 et seq. The city engineer shall include any requirements of GDOT in any action taken upon such proposed plat.
c. If a major subdivision proposes access to a Barrow County highway, the Barrow County Public Works Department shall determine the feasibility of such access. The subdivider/developer shall produce permits and/or written permission from the Barrow County Public Works Department for the proposed access.
d. The city's development team shall conduct individual reviews of the plat. The city shall review the plat for compliance with minimum preliminary plat specifications set forth at subsection (f) of this section and with any other provision of this chapter and shall forward their recommendation to the planning, zoning, and appeals board.
e. Action on preliminary plat. City council shall hold a public hearing on the preliminary plat of a subdivision. Council shall review the preliminary plat of a subdivision for conformance to the rules and regulations of this chapter, and the requirements of the zoning ordinance, and shall take into consideration the general requirements of the community and the best use of the land to be subdivided. The preliminary plat may be approved, disapproved, or approved subject to conditions or modifications. City council may indicate the specific changes required, and the amount of public improvements or the amount of the performance bond therefor which it will require as a prerequisite to the approval of the final plat.
f. Preliminary plat specifications. The preliminary plat shall be prepared by a Georgia Registered Land Surveyor; if a professional engineer contributed to the plat, then such shall be indicated upon the plat. The plat shall be drawn at a scale not smaller than two hundred (200) feet to one (1) inch. However, where the size of the proposed subdivision exceeds 100 acres, the scale may be no smaller than two hundred (200) feet to one (1) inch. The plat shall include:
1. The proposed name of the subdivision, new roads, and existing roads;
2. The name, address, and telephone number of the person to be notified of action;
3. The name, address, and license number of the registered land surveyor responsible for the preparation of the plat;
4. The graphic scale, north arrow, and date of plat preparation;
5. The total acreage in single parcel ownership by the developer/subdivider, the total number of acres being subdivided, the total number of lots created, and the total length of roads;
6. A general location map at a scale not less than one (1) inch equals one thousand (1,000) feet, showing the proposed development in relation to other adjacent properties and existing roads in City of Winder;
7. The location, zoning classification(s), and dimensions of all boundary lines of the parcel to the nearest hundredth of a foot and the deed record names of adjacent owners or subdivisions shall be identified;
8. The location and dimensions of existing rights-of-way, easements, streams, drainage structures, or utilities, buildings, bridges, lakes, and lands subject to flooding; if applicable, the 100-year floodplain should be shown;
9. Topography by contours at vertical intervals of not more than two (2) feet; prominent drainage features, such as lakes, depressions, streams, etc., which may affect subdivision design shall be shown; engineering data showing the high-water elevation and how it was established shall be submitted with the plat. The contour requirement may be waived, or the interval adjusted up or down based upon the need, as determined by the city engineer;
10. A stormwater management plan shall be submitted with technical design criteria;
11. The layout and scaled dimensions of all lots and roads; the location, purpose, and dimensions of all existing and future drainageways and easements;
12. The location and dimension of all areas proposed to be set aside for recreation use, open space, green space, or other public use, with designation of the purpose thereof, and conditions, if any, of the dedication or reservation;
13. The centerline profile of all proposed roads showing natural and finished grades and proposed road names, and a cross-section of the proposed road design;
14. Front and rear structure or building setback lines;
15. The plat must include a statement indicating the water and sewer service provider for the proposed development. This information should specify whether water service will be provided by individual water supply or public treated water, and whether sewage disposal service will be by on-site sewage management systems or sanitary sewer. The size of sanitary sewer mains and water mains shall be determined by the respective utility providers;
16. Environmentally sensitive areas: location of major river corridors, water supply watersheds, groundwater recharge areas, wetlands, the boundary, and elevation of the 100-year floodplain, as determined by past history of flooding or the best available data;
17. Where a proposed subdivision is to be developed in phases with additional plat(s) to be submitted later, a preliminary plat shall be accompanied by a future tract plan, depicting reasonably the entire lot or parcel to be divided, the future road system, and topography thereon.
(2) Final plat. Pursuant to approval of the preliminary plat by city council and land disturbance permit (LDP), a developer/subdivider shall submit a final plat, which is in conformity with the previously approved preliminary plat and LDP, to the city, pursuant to the following sequence of submittal, review, and approval:
a. Before a final plat is submitted, all public utility extensions or public or private roads which were approved in the preliminary plat shall be completed in accordance with approved City of Winder standards, and certificates of satisfactory completion have been obtained from appropriate authorities. In In lieu thereof, the developer or subdivider shall adhere to the requirements outlined in this chapter and section 28-42: Guarantees and Sureties. This may involve submitting a performance bond, an irrevocable letter of credit, or placing funds in escrow, with an amount not less than one hundred twenty (120%) percent of the estimated construction expenses required to meet the specified standards. Construction must be completed upon the issuance of the first building permit for a lot or parcel along the respective road.
b. Extensions may be granted on a case-by-case basis by the city through demonstration of progress of the improvements required by the preliminary plat.
c. Approval procedures. After preliminary plat approval and conformity with any conditions previously imposed on the preliminary plat, submittal of the final plat and all supporting documentation, data, and certifications, the final plat and/or site plan shall be approved or disapproved by the City of Winder Planning and Development Department. The City of Winder Planning and Development Department may disapprove any such final plat if any portion of the proposed subdivision is in violation of any applicable ordinance, law, rule, or regulation of any governmental unit having jurisdiction. The final plat shall be presented to the city council at the public meeting to determine whether the mayor is authorized to sign the final plat. At such public meeting, the city council shall consider the recommendation of staff and determine whether the final plat is in conformity with the preliminary plat, including any conditions imposed thereon. The city council may approve or deny the final plat.
d. Approval of the final plat and/or site plan by the City of Winder Planning and Development Department shall not be deemed an acceptance by the city or the public of the dedication of any road, right-of-way, or other real property shown upon the plat or site plan. The developer/subdivider must prepare deeds of conveyance for each right-of-way or other public space and separate action is required by the city council to accept such deeds of conveyance.
